Why AGPL and not MIT

Releases up to and including v2.1.0 were published under the MIT licence, and
those releases stay MIT: a licence already granted cannot be withdrawn, and
anyone holding that code keeps those terms. Everything from here is AGPL-3.0.

The change is about one specific thing. MIT lets someone take this, improve it,
run it as a service and never show the improvements to anyone. The scoring
curves, the sensor/model merge and the source attribution in this repository are
the parts worth having, and they were derived from measurements that volunteers
give away for free. AGPL's section 13 closes that: run a modified version where
other people can reach it over a network, and you owe them the source.

It does not stop anyone using this commercially, and it is not meant to. That is
what makes it an open source licence rather than a restriction dressed as one.

